{"id":"neet-2026-q049","pn":49,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Gas volume stoichiometry","q":"When 1 dm³ of CO₂ gas is passed over hot coke the volume of gaseous mixture after complete reaction at STP becomes 1.4 dm³. The composition of the gaseous mixture at STP is:","o":["0.6 dm³ of CO, 0.8 dm³ of CO₂","0.8 dm³ of CO, 0.8 dm³ of CO₂","0.8 dm³ of CO, 0.6 dm³ of CO₂","0.6 dm³ of CO, 0.4 dm³ of CO₂"],"c":2,"s":"The reaction is CO₂ + C(s) → 2CO.\nLet x dm³ of CO₂ react. Then CO₂ left = 1 – x and CO formed = 2x.\nTotal volume = (1 – x) + 2x = 1 + x = 1.4, so x = 0.4 dm³.\nVolume of CO₂ = 1 – 0.4 = 0.6 dm³.\nVolume of CO = 2 × 0.4 = 0.8 dm³."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q065","pn":65,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Mole concept","q":"The number of hydrogen atoms present in 5.4 g of urea is:\n(Given: Molar mass of urea : 60 g mol⁻¹, N_A : 6.022 × 10²³ particles mol⁻¹)","o":["2.168 × 10²³","2.168 × 10²²","1.084 × 10²²","1.084 × 10²³"],"c":0,"s":"Urea is H₂N–CO–NH₂, so one molecule has 4 H atoms.\nMoles of urea = 5.4/60 = 0.09.\nH atoms = 0.09 × 4 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.1679 × 10²³.\nThis is about 2.168 × 10²³.","sfig":{"w":170,"h":58,"alt":"Urea: H₂N–C–NH₂ with O double-bonded above the central C.","d":[["t",90,22,"O","m"],["l",87.5,26,87.5,38],["l",92.5,26,92.5,38],["t",90,52,"C","m"],["t",82,52,"H₂N –","e"],["t",98,52,"– NH₂",""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q075","pn":75,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Redox titration normality","q":"In an acidic medium, 10 mL of 0.25 M oxalic acid is titrated with KMnO₄ solution. If the volume of KMnO₄ solution required to reach end point is 10 mL, the strength of the KMnO₄ solution is","o":["0.20 M","0.25 M","0.15 M","0.10 M"],"c":3,"s":"Reaction: KMnO₄ + H₂C₂O₄ → Mn²⁺ + 2CO₂ + H₂O (acidic medium).\nAt the end point, equivalents of KMnO₄ = equivalents of H₂C₂O₄.\nMn⁷⁺ → Mn²⁺ gains 5 electrons, oxalic acid loses 2 electrons.\n10 × 5 × M = 10 × 0.25 × 2\nM = 5/50 = 1/10 = 0.10 M"},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q088","pn":88,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Stoichiometry, combustion","q":"The amount of carbon dioxide evolved upon complete combustion of 116 g of n-butane is\n(Given: atomic mass in amu H = 1, C = 12 and O = 16)","o":["322 g","176 g","362 g","352 g"],"c":3,"s":"C₄H₁₀ + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O.\n1 mole of C₄H₁₀ produces 4 mole of CO₂.\n58 g C₄H₁₀ gives 4 × 44 g CO₂.\nSo 116 g C₄H₁₀ gives (4 × 44 / 58) × 116 = 352 g CO₂."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q089","pn":89,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Rounding off and significant figures","q":"The numbers 17.0145 and 21.0235 were rounded to three figures after the decimal point. The resulting numbers, respectively, are","o":["17.015 and 21.023","17.014 and 21.024","17.015 and 21.024","17.014 and 21.023"],"c":1,"s":"Rule: if the digit to be removed is 5, the preceding digit stays the same if it is even.\nIf the preceding digit is odd, it is increased by one.\nIn 17.0145 the preceding digit is 4 (even), so it becomes 17.014.\nIn 21.0235 the preceding digit is 3 (odd), so it becomes 21.024."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q054","pn":54,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Dalton's atomic theory","q":"Dalton's Atomic theory could not explain which of the following?","o":["Law of conservation of mass","Law of constant proportion","Law of multiple proportion","Law of gaseous volume"],"c":3,"s":"Dalton's theory could explain the laws of chemical combination.\nHowever, it could not explain the law of gaseous volumes."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q068","pn":68,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Mole concept, number of atoms","q":"Among the following, choose the ones with equal number of atoms.\nA. 212 g of Na₂CO₃(s) [molar mass = 106 g]\nB. 248 g of Na₂O(s) [molar mass = 62 g]\nC. 240 g of NaOH(s) [molar mass = 40 g]\nD. 12 g of H₂(g) [molar mass = 2 g]\nE. 220 g of CO₂(g) [molar mass = 44 g]\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A, B, and C only","A, B, and D only","B, C, and D only","B, D, and E only"],"c":1,"s":"Number of atoms = (given mass / molar mass) × atomicity × N_A\nA. (212/106) × 6 × N_A = 12 N_A\nB. (248/62) × 3 × N_A = 12 N_A\nC. (240/40) × 3 × N_A = 18 N_A\nD. (12/2) × N_A × 2 = 12 N_A\nE. (220/44) × N_A × 3 = 15 N_A\nA, B and D have the same number of atoms."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q055","pn":55,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Limiting reagent and molarity","q":"1 gram of sodium hydroxide was treated with 25 mL of 0.75 M HCl solution, the mass of sodium hydroxide left unreacted is equal to","o":["Zero mg","200 mg","750 mg","250 mg"],"c":3,"s":"Molarity M = (W × 1000)/(M₂ × V in mL), so W = (M × M₂ × V)/1000.\nMass of HCl = (0.75 × 36.5 × 25)/1000 = 0.684 g.\nHCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O; 36.5 g HCl reacts with 40 g NaOH.\n0.684 g HCl reacts with (40/36.5) × 0.684 ≈ 0.750 g NaOH.\nNaOH left = 1 g − 0.750 g = 0.250 g = 250 mg."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q083","pn":83,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Mole concept, number of atoms","q":"The highest number of helium atoms is in","o":["4 g of helium","2.271098 L of helium at STP","4 mol of helium","4 u of helium"],"c":2,"s":"(1) 4 g of He = 4/4 = 1 mol = N_A atoms.\n(2) 2.2710982 L at STP = 2.271/22.710982 = 0.1 mol = 0.1 N_A atoms.\n(3) 4 mol of He = 4 N_A atoms.\n(4) 4 u of He = 4/4 = 1 atom.\nThe largest number of atoms is in 4 mol."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q086","pn":86,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Empirical formula","q":"A compound X contains 32% of A, 20% of B and remaining percentage of C. Then, the empirical formula of X is :\n(Given atomic masses of A = 64; B = 40; C = 32 u)","o":["AB₂C₂","ABC₄","A₂BC₂","ABC₃"],"c":3,"s":"Percentage of C = 100 – 32 – 20 = 48%.\nMoles of A = 32/64 = 1/2.\nMoles of B = 20/40 = 1/2.\nMoles of C = 48/32 = 3/2.\nDivide by the smallest (1/2): A : B : C = 1 : 1 : 3.\nEmpirical formula = ABC₃.","sfig":{"w":405,"h":130,"alt":"Table: A 32 percent, 32/64 = 1/2, (1/2)×2, 1; B 20 percent, 20/40 = 1/2, (1/2)×2, 1; C 48 percent, 48/32 = 3/2, (3/2)×2, 3.","d":[["r",10,10,386,118],["l",58,10,58,128],["l",144,10,144,128],["l",220,10,220,128],["l",320,10,320,128],["l",10,44,396,44],["l",10,72,396,72],["l",10,100,396,100],["t",34,31,"Element","m"],["t",101,25,"Mass","m"],["t",101,38,"percentage %","m"],["t",182,25,"No. of","m"],["t",182,38,"moles","m"],["t",270,25,"No. of moles/","m"],["t",270,38,"Smallest number","m"],["t",358,25,"Simple whole","m"],["t",358,38,"number","m"],["t",34,62,"A","m"],["t",101,62,"32%","m"],["t",182,62,"32/64 = 1/2","m"],["t",270,62,"(1/2) × 2","m"],["t",358,62,"= 1","m"],["t",34,90,"B","m"],["t",101,90,"20%","m"],["t",182,90,"20/40 = 1/2","m"],["t",270,90,"(1/2) × 2","m"],["t",358,90,"= 1","m"],["t",34,118,"C","m"],["t",101,118,"48%","m"],["t",182,118,"48/32 = 3/2","m"],["t",270,118,"(3/2) × 2","m"],["t",358,118,"= 3","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q057","pn":57,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Stoichiometry (mass-mass)","q":"The right option for the mass of CO₂ produced by heating 20 g of 20 % pure limestone is (Atomic mass of Ca = 40)\n[CaCO₃ --1200 K--> CaO + CO₂]","o":["1.32 g","1.12 g","1.76 g","2.64 g"],"c":2,"s":"CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.\n100 g CaCO₃ gives 44 g CO₂.\nPure CaCO₃ = 20 × 20/100 = 4 g.\nCO₂ from 4 g = (44/100) × 4 = 1.76 g."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q052","pn":52,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Significant figures","q":"The density of the solution is 2.15 g mL⁻¹, then mass of 2.5 mL solution in correct significant figures is :","o":["53.75 g","5375 × 10⁻³ g","5.4 g","5.38 g"],"c":2,"s":"In multiplication or division, the answer keeps as many significant figures as the number that has the fewest.\nMass = density × volume = 2.15 × 2.5 = 5.375.\n2.5 has only 2 significant figures, so round to 5.4 g."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q068","pn":68,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Stoichiometry with purity","q":"What mass of 95% pure CaCO₃ will be required to neutralise 50 mL of 0.5 M HCl solution according to the following reaction?\nCaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l)\n[Calculate upto second place of decimal point]","o":["1.32 g","3.65 g","9.50 g","1.25 g"],"c":0,"s":"Let m g of the impure sample be taken.\nPure CaCO₃ = (95/100) × m g.\nMoles of CaCO₃ = (95/100) × (m/100).\nMoles of HCl needed = 2 × moles of CaCO₃.\nMoles of HCl present = (50/1000) × 0.5.\nSo 2 × (95/100) × (m/100) = (50/1000) × 0.5.\nm = 1.315 g ≈ 1.32 g."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q082","pn":82,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Empirical formula","q":"An organic compound contains 78% (by wt.) carbon and remaining percentage of hydrogen. The right option for the empirical formula of this compound is : [Atomic wt. of C is 12, H is 1]","o":["CH₄","CH","CH₂","CH₃"],"c":3,"s":"Take 100 g: C = 78 g, H = 22 g.\nMoles C = 78/12 = 6.5. Moles H = 22/1 = 22.\nDivide by 6.5: C = 1, H = 22/6.5 = 3.38.\nThe nearest simple whole number ratio is 1 : 3, so the empirical formula is CH₃."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q162","pn":162,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Mole concept","q":"Which one of the followings has maximum number of atoms?","o":["1 g of Mg(s) [Atomic mass of Mg = 24]","1 g of O₂(g) [Atomic mass of O = 16]","1 g of Li(s) [Atomic mass of Li = 7]","1 g of Ag(s) [Atomic mass of Ag = 108]"],"c":2,"s":"Number of atoms = (mass/molar mass) × N_A × atoms per molecule.\nMg: 1/24 N_A.\nO₂: (1/32) × 2 N_A = 1/16 N_A.\nLi: 1/7 N_A.\nAg: 1/108 N_A.\n1/7 is the largest, so Li has the most atoms."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q162","pn":162,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Stoichiometry","q":"The number of moles of hydrogen molecules required to produce 20 moles of ammonia through Haber’s process is :","o":["30","40","10","20"],"c":0,"s":"N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃.\n3 mol H₂ give 2 mol NH₃.\nFor 20 mol NH₃: moles of H₂ = 20 × 3/2 = 30."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q057","pn":57,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","st":"Mole concept","q":"In which case is the number of molecules of water maximum ?","o":["18 mL of water","0.18 g of water","0.00224 L of water vapours at 1 atm and 273 K","10⁻³ mol of water"],"c":0,"s":"Count moles in each case.\n18 mL water = 18 g (density 1 g/mL) = 1 mol.\n0.18 g = (0.18)/(18) = 0.01 mol.\n0.00224 L at STP = (0.00224)/(22.4) = 10⁻⁴ mol.\n10⁻³ mol is given directly.\nThe largest is 1 mol, so option 1."}
